.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt GETSOCKNAME 2
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm getsockname ,
.Nm getpeername
.Nd get the name of a socket or its peer
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft int
.Fo getsockname
.Fa "int s"
.Fa "struct sockaddr *restrict name"
.Fa "socklen_t *restrict namelen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo getpeername
.Fa "int s"
.Fa "struct sockaddr *restrict name"
.Fa "socklen_t *restrict namelen"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn getsockname
system call stores the local address of the socket
.Fa s
at
.Fa name ,
and
.Fn getpeername
the address of its peer.
At most
.Fa *namelen
bytes are stored, and
.Fa *namelen
is set to the address's full length, which may be more.
.Pp
A local socket's address and its length are described in
.Xr unix 4 ;
an unbound one has an unnamed address.
An Internet socket's address is the interface address and its port, 0 if
it is not bound.
.Pp
The peer of a connection is the socket at its other end; a local socket
whose peer has closed, and a TCP socket still connecting, have none.
The peer of a datagram socket is its default destination, set by
.Xr connect 2 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa s
is not an open descriptor.
.It Bq Er ENOTCONN
.Pq Fn getpeername
The socket has no peer.
.It Bq Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa s
is not a socket.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr accept 2 ,
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr connect 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr unix 4
.Sh STANDARDS
These calls conform to
.St -p1003.1-2008 .
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn getsockname
and
.Fn getpeername
system calls first appeared in
.Bx 4.2 .
In OxideBSD they are system calls 559 and 581.
